# The weight p of the choice is a program variable, never assigned, so the bound is asked for
# every initial p >= 0.  Where p >= 2 the choice is reached with weight p outside [0, 1]: such a
# run is an error from which no bound may be proved, so against an upper bound it is worth +oo
# and the bound fails at every p >= 2, although every other run's indicator [c = 1] is at most
# 1.  Elsewhere c = 0 and the bound holds.
# Expected: unknown.  The cwlp query is reduced to a lower bound on wp(S, 1 - [c = 1]); when an
# out-of-range weight made the run abort it was worth 1 in wlp, as a diverging run is, the
# bound 1 held there, and it was proved (valid).
nat p;
nat c;
c := 0;
if (p >= 2) {
	{
		c := 1;
	}[p]{
		c := 0;
	}
} else {
	c := 0;
}
?Ex [[c = 1]] <= 1 cwlp
